Z||000000||Pyro||03-16-2000||04:34 PM||||My name is Mark, and I just got my Fiero (White '86 GT, 6-cyl, 4-spd manual) in November.  I love it so far, but it's giving me some problems.  Right before Christmas it died like it was out of gas.  After I replaced the Fuel Filter and then the Distributer Cap & Rotor, IAC, Plugs & Wires, it still wouldn't start.  So I took it to the local Pontiac Dealership.  They said it was out of gas.  I know this isn't true because I had put gas in it.  So since then, I don't let the gauge get below 1/4.  No problems since then ... until now.  It did the same thing again, and my mechanic is stumped.  He says it might be electrical.  Any advice or similar problems?  Also he says the IAC is bad again.  Is there a reason for this?  Is there a way to bypass it?  Thanks for your help and sorry for the long note.||||reg||8
Z||000001||william_thorniii||03-16-2000||05:31 PM||||Pyro,<P>You might want to check and see if your fuel gage is incorrect.  Run it low and then fill it up.  The tank should only hold slightly more than 10 Gallons (even if you have run it dry).  Then bend the needle as needed.  <P>There is no way to bypass the IAC to my knowledge.  I do know that when the engine is in diagnostic mode, the IAC should pop in and out (at least on an 88).  Perhaps you could try this.  Please note that this will not tell you if the IAC is functioning properly.  It will, however; tell you if it is functioning at all.  <P>Another possible culpret could be the cold start valve or injector.  If all else fails...<P>Fiero56, <P>Do you have any valuable input, since it is your 88 and IAC that I am referring to?<P>William T. Thorn, III  <p>[This message has been edited by william_thorniii (edited 03-16-2000).]||||reg||3
